1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,425
The Spanish Dollar That Ruled
Singapore's Early Economy

2
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:07,531
When the East India
Company finished buying

3
00:00:07,611 --> 00:00:11,162
Singapore in 1824, the
bill was written in a coin

4
00:00:11,242 --> 00:00:14,725
that belonged to neither
Britain nor Singapore.

5
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:19,344
Sultan Hussein received
33,200 Spanish dollars

6
00:00:19,424 --> 00:00:23,635
and a monthly allowance of 1,300 for life,

7
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:28,925
and the Temenggong received
26,800 and 700 a month.

8
00:00:28,925 --> 00:00:32,709
The money that priced the
island's cession had been struck

9
00:00:32,789 --> 00:00:36,173
in Mexico and Peru in the
name of the King of Spain,

10
00:00:36,253 --> 00:00:40,650
and it stayed Singapore's everyday
money for another eighty years.

11
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:44,887
The Spanish dollar, also
called the piece of eight or

12
00:00:44,967 --> 00:00:48,960
the eight-real coin, had
been struck since 1497 to

13
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:53,275
a fixed standard of about
25.6 grams of fine silver.

14
00:00:53,275 --> 00:00:57,161
Its strength was uniformity:
it was produced in huge volume

15
00:00:57,241 --> 00:01:01,329
and to a remarkably consistent
standard, so a dollar minted in

16
00:01:01,409 --> 00:01:05,375
the Americas could be trusted
far from where it was struck.

17
00:01:05,375 --> 00:01:09,678
The Manila galleons carried it
across the Pacific in bulk to the

18
00:01:09,758 --> 00:01:14,609
Philippines and China, and it is often
called the first global currency,

19
00:01:14,689 --> 00:01:18,250
accepted across Europe,
Asia and the Americas alike.

20
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:21,796
Chinese merchants stamped
small chopmarks into the

21
00:01:21,876 --> 00:01:25,575
coins they handled to vouch
for their authenticity.

22
00:01:25,575 --> 00:01:28,715
By the time the British arrived in 1819,

23
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:32,175
Singapore's traders were already using it.

24
00:01:32,175 --> 00:01:35,522
A mix of currencies was
in circulation at first,

25
00:01:35,602 --> 00:01:40,734
including Indian rupees and Dutch
guilders, but the Spanish silver dollar

26
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:45,590
quickly took over, and the Resident,
John Crawfurd, is credited with

27
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:50,025
formalising it as the settlement's
official currency in 1823.

28
00:01:50,025 --> 00:01:54,186
The Monetary Authority of
Singapore's own history describes

29
00:01:54,266 --> 00:01:57,636
the silver dollar as the
main currency of trade,

30
00:01:57,716 --> 00:02:02,100
with copper coins used mainly
at the bazaar for small change.

31
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:05,500
The Company's own
preference was different.

32
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,076
After Singapore became part of
the Straits Settlements in 1826,

33
00:02:10,156 --> 00:02:13,549
the administration pushed
for the Indian rupee,

34
00:02:13,629 --> 00:02:17,250
which was the currency of
the Company's accounts.

35
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:19,800
The market did not follow.

36
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:24,184
The Spanish dollar, by then
increasingly called the Mexican dollar,

37
00:02:24,264 --> 00:02:26,183
stayed popular with merchants,

38
00:02:26,263 --> 00:02:31,313
so the colony ran on a split arrangement:
official books were kept in rupees,

39
00:02:31,393 --> 00:02:34,125
while daily transactions were in dollars.

40
00:02:34,125 --> 00:02:37,880
The first banknote to carry
the word "Singapore" was

41
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:41,788
not government paper at all
but a note issued in 1840

42
00:02:41,868 --> 00:02:45,850
by the Singapore branch of
the Union Bank of Calcutta.

43
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:49,289
The arrangement lasted until London took

44
00:02:49,369 --> 00:02:52,625
over the Straits Settlements in 1867.

45
00:02:52,625 --> 00:02:55,876
The rupee then lost its
legal-tender status,

46
00:02:55,956 --> 00:02:59,965
and Mexican, Hong Kong,
Spanish, Peruvian and Bolivian

47
00:03:00,045 --> 00:03:03,300
silver dollars were
all recognised at once.

48
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:05,767
A colony's money was, in effect,

49
00:03:05,847 --> 00:03:09,350
whatever silver coins
its merchants trusted.

50
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:14,275
That worked as long as the supply
of good silver dollars held up.

51
00:03:14,275 --> 00:03:19,451
As it dwindled in the 1890s, the
government began issuing its own

52
00:03:19,531 --> 00:03:24,789
British trade dollars in 1895,
created a Board of Commissioners of

53
00:03:24,869 --> 00:03:30,125
Currency in 1897, and issued its
first banknotes soon afterwards.

54
00:03:30,125 --> 00:03:34,819
In 1903 the board issued the
Straits dollar, valued at par with

55
00:03:34,899 --> 00:03:39,441
the trade dollar, and on the
31st of August 1904 the British,

56
00:03:39,521 --> 00:03:44,825
Mexican and Hong Kong dollars ceased
to be legal tender in the colony.

57
00:03:44,825 --> 00:03:47,924
A coin whose value was
its silver could not be

58
00:03:48,004 --> 00:03:51,242
steadier than the silver
price, and the colony's

59
00:03:51,322 --> 00:03:54,225
exchange rate with sterling moved with it.

60
00:03:54,225 --> 00:03:58,502
In 1906 the Straits dollar was
therefore tied to gold instead,

61
00:03:58,582 --> 00:04:01,172
fixed at two shillings and four pence,

62
00:04:01,252 --> 00:04:06,100
with the British gold sovereign made
legal tender for the first time.

63
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:08,829
The peg answered silver's instability,

64
00:04:08,909 --> 00:04:13,634
but silver itself kept moving: as
its price rose, the coin risked

65
00:04:13,714 --> 00:04:17,256
becoming worth more melted
than spent, and a new,

66
00:04:17,336 --> 00:04:21,550
smaller dollar with less silver
in it was issued in 1907.

67
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:25,399
The Straits dollar moved
onto a sterling exchange

68
00:04:25,479 --> 00:04:29,649
standard when Britain left
the gold standard in 1931,

69
00:04:29,729 --> 00:04:34,300
and in 1939 it was replaced
at par by the Malayan dollar.

70
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,789
Singapore's story is a small
window onto a larger one:

71
00:04:37,869 --> 00:04:42,417
the role of the world's money has
passed from one currency to another,

72
00:04:42,497 --> 00:04:46,000
and the island happened to
sit under several of them.

73
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,381
The Spanish dollar was the first
true global reserve currency,

74
00:04:50,461 --> 00:04:53,763
recognised from the
sixteenth to the nineteenth

75
00:04:53,843 --> 00:04:57,225
centuries across Europe,
Asia and the Americas.

76
00:04:57,225 --> 00:05:00,642
As commerce shifted north
in the seventeenth century,

77
00:05:00,722 --> 00:05:04,668
the Dutch guilder became the
main currency between Europe and

78
00:05:04,748 --> 00:05:08,575
the Dutch colonial empire,
through the eighteenth century.

79
00:05:08,575 --> 00:05:12,740
In the nineteenth century
the pound sterling took over,

80
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:15,595
and by the late 1800s more than sixty

81
00:05:15,675 --> 00:05:19,225
percent of world trade
was invoiced in pounds.

82
00:05:19,225 --> 00:05:22,889
At the Bretton Woods
conference in 1944 the US

83
00:05:22,969 --> 00:05:27,365
dollar was only the second
currency in global reserves,

84
00:05:27,445 --> 00:05:30,375
but it soon became the dominant one.

85
00:05:30,375 --> 00:05:35,896
By the 1950s 55 percent of global
reserves were still held in sterling,

86
00:05:35,976 --> 00:05:41,025
though the share was about 10
percent lower within twenty years.

87
00:05:41,025 --> 00:05:46,024
The US dollar even inherited its
shape from the coin in this story:

88
00:05:46,104 --> 00:05:49,814
when the United States set
its own dollar in 1792,

89
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:53,073
it based it on the
Spanish dollar's weight,

90
00:05:53,153 --> 00:05:57,550
and the Spanish coin stayed
legal tender there until 1857.

91
00:05:57,550 --> 00:06:01,165
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has never

92
00:06:01,245 --> 00:06:04,450
issued the world's reserve
currency, but for two

93
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:08,487
centuries its economy has run
on whichever one was current,

94
00:06:08,567 --> 00:06:12,125
and its own money was
repeatedly redefined to match.

95
00:06:12,125 --> 00:06:16,054
The Spanish dollar that paid
for the island in 1824 was,

96
00:06:16,134 --> 00:06:20,993
at that moment, the closest thing
the world had to a common currency;

97
00:06:21,073 --> 00:06:24,072
by the time it disappeared
from Singapore's

98
00:06:24,152 --> 00:06:27,579
pockets in 1904, that role
belonged to the pound,

99
00:06:27,659 --> 00:06:31,525
and within another forty
years to the American dollar.
